<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Menzies</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1894">1894</a>–<a href="/years/1978">1978</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Robert Menzies was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>'s longest-serving prime minister, in office from 1939 to 1941 and from 1949 to 1966. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a> in 1944. His post-war government presided over a long economic boom and large-scale immigration from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anzus">ANZUS</a> treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, and expanded universities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-menzies.jpg" alt="Robert Menzies" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Library of Australia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_Menzies_1950s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert Gordon Menzies was born on 20 December 1894 at his parents' home in Jeparit, Victoria, the fourth of five children. He was the first Australian prime minister with two Australian-born parents. At 13 he came first in the state-wide scholarship examinations, which paid for his secondary education. He studied law at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>, was admitted to the Victorian Bar in 1918, specialised in constitutional law, and became a King's Counsel in 1929.</p><p>He entered the Victorian Legislative Council in 1928 for the Nationalist Party and was Deputy Premier of Victoria from 1932 to 1934. In 1934 he moved to federal politics as member for Kooyong and became Attorney-General in the government of Joseph Lyons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyons</a> died on 7 April 1939, Menzies was elected leader of the United Australia Party and was sworn in as prime minister eight days after his election on 18 April. On 3 September 1939 he announced that Australia was at war alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. With his position in his party weakening, he resigned on 27 August 1941. In opposition he spoke on radio to those he called the Forgotten People, and in October 1944 he called a conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canberra">Canberra</a> at which fourteen anti-Labor parties agreed to merge as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-of-australia">Liberal Party of Australia</a>.</p><p>He returned as prime minister on 19 December 1949 and won seven consecutive elections. His government signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anzus">ANZUS</a> treaty on 1 September 1951, sent troops to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>, maintained the post-war immigration scheme, introduced Commonwealth scholarships in 1951 and established the National Capital Development Commission in 1957. A 1950 bill to ban the Communist Party was ruled unconstitutional, and a 1951 referendum on the question was narrowly defeated. He retired in January 1966.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Menzies suffered strokes in 1971 and 1972 and used a wheelchair outside his home. He died of a heart attack at his home in Malvern, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>, on 15 May 1978. His combined term of 18 years and 163 days is the longest in Australian history, and his second period is the longest unbroken tenure in the office. He lost the two-party-preferred vote at three elections, in 1940, 1954 and 1961, while retaining government each time.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert Menzies” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Politics has been dominated by the Australian Labor Party, founded in the 1890s, and by a coalition of the Liberal Party, formed under <b>Robert Menzies</b> in 1944–1945, and the Country, later National, Party; Menzies was the longest-serving prime minister, in 1939–1941 and 1949–1966.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jeparit, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-36.13,141.98&amp;year=1948" aria-label="Jeparit on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8126 3468 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Robert Menzies"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8576 3738h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8576 3738h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-36.13,141.98&amp;year=1948">What was here in 1948? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
